-- title:
--   Continuous section functions and the orbital integral formula over a field
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a field carrying a topology making it a Hausdorff, locally compact, second-countable topological ring; $\mathrm{GL}_2(A)$ and the centraliser of a point are equipped with their Borel $\sigma$-algebras `glBorelOf` and `centralizerBorel`, that is, the $\sigma$-algebras generated by their topologies. Let $\mu$ be a Haar measure on $\mathrm{GL}_2(A)$, let $\gamma \in \mathrm{GL}_2(A)$ be regular semisimple in the sense that $\operatorname{tr}(\gamma)^2 - 4\det(\gamma)$ is a unit of $A$, let $\tau$ be a Haar measure on the centraliser subgroup $Z_\gamma = \mathrm{Cent}(\{\gamma\})$, and let $f : \mathrm{GL}_2(A) \to \mathbb{C}$ be Borel measurable, of compact support, and bounded (some real $C$ satisfies $\|f(g)\| \le C$ for all $g$). Call $w : \mathrm{GL}_2(A) \to \mathbb{R}$ a section function for $(\gamma, \tau, f)$ when $w \ge 0$, $w$ is Borel measurable with compact support, and $\int_{Z_\gamma} w(tx)\, d\tau(t) = 1$ for every $x$ with $f(x^{-1}\gamma x) \ne 0$. The conclusion is twofold: there exists a continuous section function for $(\gamma,\tau,f)$; and for every section function $w$ and every $I \in \mathbb{C}$, the assertion that $I$ is an orbital integral of $f$ at $\gamma$ relative to $(\mu,\tau)$ — i.e. that $I = \int f(x^{-1}\gamma x) v(x)\, d\mu(x)$ for some section function $v$ — holds if and only if $I = \int f(x^{-1}\gamma x) w(x)\, d\mu(x)$.
--
--   This is the well-definedness statement for the regular semisimple local orbital integral of $f$ at $\gamma$ over a locally compact field: a continuous truncation transverse to the centraliser orbits exists, and the resulting integral does not depend on which section function is used. It is invoked by the comparison of orbital integrals at regular semisimple elements and by the construction of twisted orbital integrals at norm conjugators over $\mathbb{R}$, $\mathbb{C}$ and the non-archimedean completions.
